1
00:00:03,438 --> 00:00:07,438
After well considering the matter while I was dressing at the Blue Boar in the morning,

2
00:00:07,438 --> 00:00:12,120
I resolved to tell my guardian that I doubted Orlick’s being the right sort of man to fill a

3
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:18,680
post of trust at Miss Havisham’s. “Why of course he is not the right sort of man, Pip,” said my

4
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:24,559
guardian, comfortably satisfied beforehand on the general head, “because the man who fills the

5
00:00:24,559 --> 00:00:31,358
post of trust never is the right sort of man.” It seemed quite to put him into spirits to find that

6
00:00:31,358 --> 00:00:37,597
this particular post was not exceptionally held by the right sort of man, and he listened in a

7
00:00:37,597 --> 00:00:44,718
satisfied manner while I told him what knowledge I had of Orlick. “Very good, Pip,” he observed,

8
00:00:44,718 --> 00:00:51,120
when I had concluded, “I’ll go round presently, and pay our friend off.” Rather alarmed by this

9
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:56,319
summary action, I was for a little delay, and even hinted that our friend himself might be

10
00:00:56,319 --> 00:01:03,038
difficult to deal with. “Oh no he won’t,” said my guardian, making his pocket-handkerchief-point,

11
00:01:03,038 --> 00:01:10,000
with perfect confidence; “I should like to see him argue the question with me.”

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,038
As we were going back together to London by the midday coach, and as I breakfasted under such

13
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:21,000
terrors of Pumblechook that I could scarcely hold my cup, this gave me an opportunity of saying that

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,718
I wanted a walk, and that I would go on along the London road while Mr. Jaggers was occupied,

15
00:01:26,718 --> 00:01:32,160
if he would let the coachman know that I would get into my place when overtaken. I was thus enabled

16
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:37,718
to fly from the Blue Boar immediately after breakfast. By then making a loop of about a

17
00:01:37,718 --> 00:01:42,758
couple of miles into the open country at the back of Pumblechook’s premises, I got round

18
00:01:42,758 --> 00:01:50,237
into the High Street again, a little beyond that pitfall, and felt myself in comparative security.

19
00:01:50,237 --> 00:01:54,436
It was interesting to be in the quiet old town once more, and it was not disagreeable

20
00:01:54,438 --> 00:02:01,000
to be here and there suddenly recognised and stared after. One or two of the tradespeople

21
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,597
even darted out of their shops and went a little way down the street before me,

22
00:02:05,597 --> 00:02:12,319
that they might turn, as if they had forgotten something, and pass me face to face,—on which

23
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:18,639
occasions I don’t know whether they or I made the worse pretence; they of not doing it,

24
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:24,438
or I of not seeing it. Still my position was a distinguished one, and I was not at

25
00:02:24,438 --> 00:02:32,597
all dissatisfied with it, until Fate threw me in the way of that unlimited miscreant, Trabb’s boy.

26
00:02:32,597 --> 00:02:38,160
Casting my eyes along the street at a certain point of my progress, I beheld Trabb’s boy

27
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:44,519
approaching, lashing himself with an empty blue bag. Deeming that a serene and unconscious

28
00:02:44,519 --> 00:02:51,800
contemplation of him would best beseem me, and would be most likely to quell his evil mind, I

29
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:58,120
advanced with that expression of countenance, and was rather congratulating myself on my success,

30
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:04,120
when suddenly the knees of Trabb’s boy smote together, his hair uprose, his cap fell off,

31
00:03:04,120 --> 00:03:09,641
he trembled violently in every limb, staggered out into the road, and crying to the populace,

32
00:03:09,641 --> 00:03:15,480
“Hold me! I’m so frightened!” feigned to be in a paroxysm of terror and contrition,

33
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:21,358
occasioned by the dignity of my appearance. As I passed him, his teeth loudly chattered

34
00:03:21,358 --> 00:03:28,358
in his head, and with every mark of extreme humiliation, he prostrated himself in the dust.

35
00:03:28,358 --> 00:03:33,838
This was a hard thing to bear, but this was nothing. I had not advanced another

36
00:03:33,839 --> 00:03:39,597
two hundred yards when, to my inexpressible terror, amazement, and indignation,

37
00:03:39,597 --> 00:03:45,319
I again beheld Trabb’s boy approaching. He was coming round a narrow corner. His blue

38
00:03:45,319 --> 00:03:51,436
bag was slung over his shoulder, honest industry beamed in his eyes, a determination to proceed

39
00:03:51,438 --> 00:03:58,160
to Trabb’s with cheerful briskness was indicated in his gait. With a shock he became aware of me,

40
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:05,237
and was severely visited as before; but this time his motion was rotatory, and he staggered round

41
00:04:05,237 --> 00:04:12,277
and round me with knees more afflicted, and with uplifted hands as if beseeching for mercy. His

42
00:04:12,277 --> 00:04:19,639
sufferings were hailed with the greatest joy by a knot of spectators, and I felt utterly confounded.

43
00:04:19,641 --> 00:04:24,718
I had not got as much further down the street as the post-office, when I again beheld Trabb’s boy

44
00:04:24,718 --> 00:04:30,879
shooting round by a back way. This time, he was entirely changed. He wore the blue

45
00:04:30,879 --> 00:04:35,597
bag in the manner of my great-coat, and was strutting along the pavement towards me on

46
00:04:35,597 --> 00:04:40,519
the opposite side of the street, attended by a company of delighted young friends to whom he

47
00:04:40,519 --> 00:04:46,879
from time to time exclaimed, with a wave of his hand, “Don’t know yah!” Words cannot state the

48
00:04:46,879 --> 00:04:53,120
amount of aggravation and injury wreaked upon me by Trabb’s boy, when passing abreast of me,

49
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:58,038
he pulled up his shirt-collar, twined his side-hair, stuck an arm akimbo,

50
00:04:58,038 --> 00:05:04,277
and smirked extravagantly by, wriggling his elbows and body, and drawling to his attendants,

51
00:05:04,277 --> 00:05:10,277
“Don’t know yah, don’t know yah, ’pon my soul don’t know yah!” The disgrace attendant on

52
00:05:10,277 --> 00:05:15,277
his immediately afterwards taking to crowing and pursuing me across the bridge with crows,

53
00:05:15,277 --> 00:05:21,120
as from an exceedingly dejected fowl who had known me when I was a blacksmith, culminated

54
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:29,199
the disgrace with which I left the town, and was, so to speak, ejected by it into the open country.

55
00:05:29,199 --> 00:05:35,319
But unless I had taken the life of Trabb’s boy on that occasion, I really do not even now see what

56
00:05:35,319 --> 00:05:41,639
I could have done save endure. To have struggled with him in the street, or to have exacted any

57
00:05:41,641 --> 00:05:48,800
lower recompense from him than his heart’s best blood, would have been futile and degrading.

58
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:55,438
Moreover, he was a boy whom no man could hurt; an invulnerable and dodging serpent who,

59
00:05:55,438 --> 00:06:01,879
when chased into a corner, flew out again between his captor’s legs, scornfully yelping. I wrote,

60
00:06:01,879 --> 00:06:06,839
however, to Mr. Trabb by next day’s post, to say that Mr. Pip must decline to deal further with

61
00:06:06,839 --> 00:06:12,319
one who could so far forget what he owed to the best interests of society, as to employ a boy who

62
00:06:12,319 --> 00:06:19,078
excited Loathing in every respectable mind. The coach, with Mr. Jaggers inside, came up

63
00:06:19,078 --> 00:06:26,800
in due time, and I took my box-seat again, and arrived in London safe,—but not sound, for my

64
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:34,319
heart was gone. As soon as I arrived, I sent a penitential codfish and barrel of oysters to Joe

65
00:06:34,319 --> 00:06:39,758
(as reparation for not having gone myself), and then went on to Barnard’s Inn.

66
00:06:39,758 --> 00:06:46,120
I found Herbert dining on cold meat, and delighted to welcome me back. Having despatched The Avenger

67
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:50,917
to the coffee-house for an addition to the dinner, I felt that I must open my breast that very

68
00:06:50,917 --> 00:06:57,438
evening to my friend and chum. As confidence was out of the question with The Avenger in the hall,

69
00:06:57,438 --> 00:07:02,917
which could merely be regarded in the light of an antechamber to the keyhole, I sent him to the

70
00:07:02,917 --> 00:07:11,641
Play. A better proof of the severity of my bondage to that taskmaster could scarcely be afforded,

71
00:07:11,641 --> 00:07:17,519
than the degrading shifts to which I was constantly driven to find him employment.

72
00:07:17,519 --> 00:07:24,199
So mean is extremity, that I sometimes sent him to Hyde Park corner to see what o’clock it was.

73
00:07:24,199 --> 00:07:29,519
Dinner done and we sitting with our feet upon the fender, I said to Herbert,

74
00:07:29,519 --> 00:07:33,917
“My dear Herbert, I have something very particular to tell you.”

75
00:07:33,917 --> 00:07:39,597
“My dear Handel,” he returned, “I shall esteem and respect your confidence.”

76
00:07:39,597 --> 00:07:44,160
“It concerns myself, Herbert,” said I, “and one other person.”

77
00:07:44,160 --> 00:07:47,758
Herbert crossed his feet, looked at the fire with his head on one side,

78
00:07:47,758 --> 00:07:53,160
and having looked at it in vain for some time, looked at me because I didn’t go on.

79
00:07:53,160 --> 00:08:01,597
“Herbert,” said I, laying my hand upon his knee, “I love—I adore—Estella.”

80
00:08:01,597 --> 00:08:08,480
Instead of being transfixed, Herbert replied in an easy matter-of-course way, “Exactly. Well?”

81
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:12,756
“Well, Herbert? Is that all you say? Well?”

82
00:08:12,758 --> 00:08:16,680
“What next, I mean?” said Herbert. “Of course I know that.”

83
00:08:16,680 --> 00:08:18,800
“How do you know it?” said I.

84
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,319
“How do I know it, Handel? Why, from you.”

85
00:08:21,319 --> 00:08:23,120
“I never told you.”

86
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:27,597
“Told me! You have never told me when you have got your hair cut,

87
00:08:27,597 --> 00:08:33,961
but I have had senses to perceive it. You have always adored her, ever since I have known you.

88
00:08:33,961 --> 00:08:39,961
You brought your adoration and your portmanteau here together. Told me! Why,

89
00:08:39,961 --> 00:08:45,559
you have always told me all day long. When you told me your own story, you told me plainly that

90
00:08:45,559 --> 00:08:51,839
you began adoring her the first time you saw her, when you were very young indeed.”

91
00:08:51,839 --> 00:08:57,680
“Very well, then,” said I, to whom this was a new and not unwelcome light, “I have never

92
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:04,078
left off adoring her. And she has come back, a most beautiful and most elegant creature.

93
00:09:04,078 --> 00:09:10,961
And I saw her yesterday. And if I adored her before, I now doubly adore her.”

94
00:09:10,961 --> 00:09:16,319
“Lucky for you then, Handel,” said Herbert, “that you are picked out for her and allotted to her.

95
00:09:16,319 --> 00:09:20,718
Without encroaching on forbidden ground, we may venture to say that there can be no doubt between

96
00:09:20,718 --> 00:09:27,277
ourselves of that fact. Have you any idea yet, of Estella’s views on the adoration question?”

97
00:09:27,277 --> 00:09:33,639
I shook my head gloomily. “Oh! She is thousands of miles away, from me,” said I.

98
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:39,639
“Patience, my dear Handel: time enough, time enough. But you have something more to say?”

99
00:09:39,641 --> 00:09:43,597
“I am ashamed to say it,” I returned, “and yet it’s no worse to say it than

100
00:09:43,597 --> 00:09:48,718
to think it. You call me a lucky fellow. Of course, I am. I was a

101
00:09:48,718 --> 00:09:55,879
blacksmith’s boy but yesterday; I am—what shall I say I am—to-day?”

102
00:09:55,879 --> 00:10:00,038
“Say a good fellow, if you want a phrase,” returned Herbert, smiling,

103
00:10:00,038 --> 00:10:06,237
and clapping his hand on the back of mine—“a good fellow, with impetuosity and hesitation,

104
00:10:06,237 --> 00:10:11,879
boldness and diffidence, action and dreaming, curiously mixed in him.”

105
00:10:11,879 --> 00:10:16,519
I stopped for a moment to consider whether there really was this mixture in my character.

106
00:10:16,519 --> 00:10:23,078
On the whole, I by no means recognised the analysis, but thought it not worth disputing.

107
00:10:23,078 --> 00:10:28,358
“When I ask what I am to call myself to-day, Herbert,” I went on, “I suggest what I have

108
00:10:28,358 --> 00:10:34,277
in my thoughts. You say I am lucky. I know I have done nothing to raise myself in life, and

109
00:10:34,277 --> 00:10:40,639
that Fortune alone has raised me; that is being very lucky. And yet, when I think of Estella—”

110
00:10:40,641 --> 00:10:44,917
(“And when don’t you, you know?” Herbert threw in, with his eyes on the fire;

111
00:10:44,917 --> 00:10:51,917
which I thought kind and sympathetic of him.) “—Then, my dear Herbert, I cannot tell you how

112
00:10:51,917 --> 00:10:59,480
dependent and uncertain I feel, and how exposed to hundreds of chances. Avoiding forbidden ground,

113
00:10:59,480 --> 00:11:06,000
as you did just now, I may still say that on the constancy of one person (naming no person)

114
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:12,319
all my expectations depend. And at the best, how indefinite and unsatisfactory, only to know so

115
00:11:12,319 --> 00:11:19,078
vaguely what they are!” In saying this, I relieved my mind of what had always been there, more or

116
00:11:19,078 --> 00:11:25,319
less, though no doubt most since yesterday. “Now, Handel,” Herbert replied, in his gay,

117
00:11:25,319 --> 00:11:30,120
hopeful way, “it seems to me that in the despondency of the tender passion,

118
00:11:30,120 --> 00:11:36,038
we are looking into our gift-horse’s mouth with a magnifying-glass. Likewise, it seems to me that,

119
00:11:36,038 --> 00:11:40,597
concentrating our attention on the examination, we altogether overlook one of the best points

120
00:11:40,597 --> 00:11:45,519
of the animal. Didn’t you tell me that your guardian, Mr. Jaggers, told you in the beginning,

121
00:11:45,519 --> 00:11:50,961
that you were not endowed with expectations only? And even if he had not told you so,—though that

122
00:11:50,961 --> 00:11:56,519
is a very large If, I grant,—could you believe that of all men in London, Mr. Jaggers is the

123
00:11:56,519 --> 00:12:01,597
man to hold his present relations towards you unless he were sure of his ground?”

124
00:12:01,597 --> 00:12:07,160
I said I could not deny that this was a strong point. I said it (people often do so,

125
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:15,078
in such cases) like a rather reluctant concession to truth and justice;—as if I wanted to deny it!

126
00:12:15,078 --> 00:12:19,680
“I should think it was a strong point,” said Herbert, “and I should think you would be puzzled

127
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:26,758
to imagine a stronger; as to the rest, you must bide your guardian’s time, and he must bide his

128
00:12:26,758 --> 00:12:32,319
client’s time. You’ll be one-and-twenty before you know where you are, and then perhaps you’ll get

129
00:12:32,319 --> 00:12:40,961
some further enlightenment. At all events, you’ll be nearer getting it, for it must come at last.”

130
00:12:40,961 --> 00:12:47,277
“What a hopeful disposition you have!” said I, gratefully admiring his cheery ways.

131
00:12:47,277 --> 00:12:53,000
“I ought to have,” said Herbert, “for I have not much else. I must acknowledge, by the by,

132
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,879
that the good sense of what I have just said is not my own, but my father’s. The only remark I

133
00:12:58,879 --> 00:13:03,718
ever heard him make on your story, was the final one, “The thing is settled and done,

134
00:13:03,718 --> 00:13:09,480
or Mr. Jaggers would not be in it.” And now before I say anything more about my father,

135
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:13,756
or my father’s son, and repay confidence with confidence,

136
00:13:13,758 --> 00:13:19,680
I want to make myself seriously disagreeable to you for a moment,—positively repulsive.”

137
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:22,559
“You won’t succeed,” said I.

138
00:13:22,559 --> 00:13:29,559
“O yes I shall!” said he. “One, two, three, and now I am in for it. Handel, my good

139
00:13:29,559 --> 00:13:36,597
fellow;”—though he spoke in this light tone, he was very much in earnest,—“I have been thinking

140
00:13:36,597 --> 00:13:42,358
since we have been talking with our feet on this fender, that Estella surely cannot be a condition

141
00:13:42,358 --> 00:13:48,756
of your inheritance, if she was never referred to by your guardian. Am I right in so understanding

142
00:13:48,758 --> 00:13:55,120
what you have told me, as that he never referred to her, directly or indirectly, in any way?

143
00:13:55,120 --> 00:14:01,398
Never even hinted, for instance, that your patron might have views as to your marriage ultimately?”

144
00:14:01,398 --> 00:14:02,877
“Never.”

145
00:14:02,879 --> 00:14:09,160
“Now, Handel, I am quite free from the flavour of sour grapes, upon my soul and honour! Not

146
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:16,319
being bound to her, can you not detach yourself from her?—I told you I should be disagreeable.”

147
00:14:16,319 --> 00:14:22,358
I turned my head aside, for, with a rush and a sweep, like the old marsh winds coming up

148
00:14:22,358 --> 00:14:27,917
from the sea, a feeling like that which had subdued me on the morning when I left the forge,

149
00:14:27,917 --> 00:14:33,319
when the mists were solemnly rising, and when I laid my hand upon the village finger-post,

150
00:14:33,319 --> 00:14:39,199
smote upon my heart again. There was silence between us for a little while.

151
00:14:39,199 --> 00:14:45,038
“Yes; but my dear Handel,” Herbert went on, as if we had been talking, instead of silent,

152
00:14:45,038 --> 00:14:50,120
“its having been so strongly rooted in the breast of a boy whom nature and circumstances made so

153
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:57,319
romantic, renders it very serious. Think of her bringing-up, and think of Miss Havisham. Think

154
00:14:57,319 --> 00:15:05,078
of what she is herself (now I am repulsive and you abominate me). This may lead to miserable things.”

155
00:15:05,078 --> 00:15:11,120
“I know it, Herbert,” said I, with my head still turned away, “but I can’t help it.”

156
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:13,319
“You can’t detach yourself?”

157
00:15:13,319 --> 00:15:15,557
“No. Impossible!”

158
00:15:15,559 --> 00:15:17,879
“You can’t try, Handel?”

159
00:15:17,879 --> 00:15:20,319
“No. Impossible!”

160
00:15:20,319 --> 00:15:24,078
“Well!” said Herbert, getting up with a lively shake as if he had been asleep,

161
00:15:24,078 --> 00:15:30,160
and stirring the fire, “now I’ll endeavour to make myself agreeable again!”

162
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:33,319
So he went round the room and shook the curtains out,

163
00:15:33,319 --> 00:15:38,319
put the chairs in their places, tidied the books and so forth that were lying about,

164
00:15:38,319 --> 00:15:42,398
looked into the hall, peeped into the letter-box, shut the door,

165
00:15:42,398 --> 00:15:49,597
and came back to his chair by the fire: where he sat down, nursing his left leg in both arms.

166
00:15:49,597 --> 00:15:55,358
“I was going to say a word or two, Handel, concerning my father and my father’s son.

167
00:15:55,358 --> 00:15:59,718
I am afraid it is scarcely necessary for my father’s son to remark that my father’s

168
00:15:59,718 --> 00:16:03,239
establishment is not particularly brilliant in its housekeeping.”

169
00:16:04,718 --> 00:16:09,398
“There is always plenty, Herbert,” said I, to say something encouraging.

170
00:16:09,398 --> 00:16:14,718
“O yes! and so the dustman says, I believe, with the strongest approval, and so does the

171
00:16:14,718 --> 00:16:21,199
marine-store shop in the back street. Gravely, Handel, for the subject is grave enough, you know

172
00:16:21,199 --> 00:16:27,480
how it is as well as I do. I suppose there was a time once when my father had not given matters up;

173
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:33,639
but if ever there was, the time is gone. May I ask you if you have ever had an opportunity

174
00:16:33,641 --> 00:16:37,839
of remarking, down in your part of the country, that the children of

175
00:16:37,839 --> 00:16:44,237
not exactly suitable marriages are always most particularly anxious to be married?”

176
00:16:44,237 --> 00:16:50,839
This was such a singular question, that I asked him in return, “Is it so?”

177
00:16:50,839 --> 00:16:56,038
“I don’t know,” said Herbert, “that’s what I want to know. Because it is decidedly the

178
00:16:56,038 --> 00:17:02,358
case with us. My poor sister Charlotte, who was next me and died before she was fourteen,

179
00:17:02,358 --> 00:17:09,879
was a striking example. Little Jane is the same. In her desire to be matrimonially established,

180
00:17:09,879 --> 00:17:15,237
you might suppose her to have passed her short existence in the perpetual contemplation of

181
00:17:15,237 --> 00:17:20,597
domestic bliss. Little Alick in a frock has already made arrangements for his union with

182
00:17:20,597 --> 00:17:27,879
a suitable young person at Kew. And indeed, I think we are all engaged, except the baby.”

183
00:17:27,879 --> 00:17:30,358
“Then you are?” said I.

184
00:17:30,358 --> 00:17:34,678
“I am,” said Herbert; “but it’s a secret.”

185
00:17:34,680 --> 00:17:36,961
I assured him of my keeping the secret,

186
00:17:36,961 --> 00:17:41,800
and begged to be favoured with further particulars. He had spoken so sensibly

187
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:46,758
and feelingly of my weakness that I wanted to know something about his strength.

188
00:17:46,758 --> 00:17:49,398
“May I ask the name?” I said.

189
00:17:49,398 --> 00:17:51,756
“Name of Clara,” said Herbert.

190
00:17:51,758 --> 00:17:53,398
“Live in London?”

191
00:17:53,398 --> 00:17:56,358
“Yes, perhaps I ought to mention,” said Herbert,

192
00:17:56,358 --> 00:18:02,160
who had become curiously crestfallen and meek, since we entered on the interesting theme,

193
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:07,718
“that she is rather below my mother’s nonsensical family notions. Her father had to do with the

194
00:18:07,718 --> 00:18:12,879
victualling of passenger-ships. I think he was a species of purser.”

195
00:18:12,879 --> 00:18:15,199
“What is he now?” said I.

196
00:18:15,199 --> 00:18:18,038
“He’s an invalid now,” replied Herbert.

197
00:18:18,038 --> 00:18:19,519
“Living on—?”

198
00:18:19,519 --> 00:18:23,641
“On the first floor,” said Herbert. Which was not at all what I meant,

199
00:18:23,641 --> 00:18:28,559
for I had intended my question to apply to his means. “I have never seen him,

200
00:18:28,559 --> 00:18:34,199
for he has always kept his room overhead, since I have known Clara. But I have heard him constantly.

201
00:18:34,199 --> 00:18:41,078
He makes tremendous rows,—roars, and pegs at the floor with some frightful instrument.” In

202
00:18:41,078 --> 00:18:47,078
looking at me and then laughing heartily, Herbert for the time recovered his usual lively manner.

203
00:18:47,078 --> 00:18:49,758
“Don’t you expect to see him?” said I.

204
00:18:49,758 --> 00:18:53,438
“O yes, I constantly expect to see him,” returned Herbert,

205
00:18:53,438 --> 00:18:55,597
“because I never hear him, without expecting him

206
00:18:55,597 --> 00:19:01,120
to come tumbling through the ceiling. But I don’t know how long the rafters may hold.”

207
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:03,038
When he had once more laughed heartily,

208
00:19:03,597 --> 00:19:08,879
he became meek again, and told me that the moment he began to realise Capital,

209
00:19:08,879 --> 00:19:14,879
it was his intention to marry this young lady. He added as a self-evident proposition,

210
00:19:14,879 --> 00:19:21,839
engendering low spirits, “But you can’t marry, you know, while you’re looking about you.”

211
00:19:21,839 --> 00:19:26,038
As we contemplated the fire, and as I thought what a difficult vision to

212
00:19:26,038 --> 00:19:33,078
realise this same Capital sometimes was, I put my hands in my pockets. A folded piece

213
00:19:33,078 --> 00:19:37,597
of paper in one of them attracting my attention, I opened it and found it

214
00:19:37,597 --> 00:19:42,961
to be the play-bill I had received from Joe, relative to the celebrated provincial amateur

215
00:19:42,961 --> 00:19:51,961
of Roscian renown. “And bless my heart,” I involuntarily added aloud, “it’s to-night!”

216
00:19:51,961 --> 00:19:57,800
This changed the subject in an instant, and made us hurriedly resolve to go to the play. So, when

217
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:02,559
I had pledged myself to comfort and abet Herbert in the affair of his heart by all practicable and

218
00:20:02,559 --> 00:20:08,961
impracticable means, and when Herbert had told me that his affianced already knew me by reputation

219
00:20:08,961 --> 00:20:13,718
and that I should be presented to her, and when we had warmly shaken hands upon our mutual

220
00:20:13,718 --> 00:20:20,199
confidence, we blew out our candles, made up our fire, locked our door, and issued forth in quest

221
00:20:20,199 --> 00:20:22,991
of Mr. Wopsle and Denmark. Chapter 31.
